      "Slower-digesting blend with a milkshake texture, buyers report",
      "Budget-tier cost per serving",
      "Consumer Reports (Oct 2025) listed it among its better choices on lead"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 190-200 calories for only about 22 g of protein",
      "Artificial sweeteners and added oils",
      "No sport certification confirmed"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to drink a thick, filling bedtime shake, as long as the extra calories fit your goal.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per scoop": "About 22 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 190-200",
      "Protein type": "Whey, casein, milk, egg",
      "Best for": "Gaining weight on a budget"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "fairlife-core-power-26g",
    "name": "Fairlife Core Power High Protein Shake (26 g)",
    "brand": "Fairlife",
    "badge": "Best Ready-to-Drink",
    "category": "Ultra-filtered milk shake",
    "description": "Core Power is ultra-filtered milk, so its protein is roughly the natural milk split of about 80% casein and 20% whey. Per the US label at the time of writing, a 14 fl oz chocolate bottle gives 26 g of protein in 170 calories, with 8 g of carbohydrate, 5 g of sugar and 0 g added sugar, and it is lactose-free. For people who will not mix a shake at 10 p.m., a cold bottle from the fridge is the path of least resistance.",
    "pros": [
      "Milk-protein blend that is mostly casein by nature",
      "No mixing; lactose-free, per the brand",
      "About 670 mg of calcium per bottle, per Fairlife"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs far more per gram of protein than powder",
      "Uses non-nutritive sweeteners; check the current label",
      "14 fl oz is a lot of liquid right before lying down"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The no-effort bedtime option, best for people who keep skipping their shake because mixing it is a chore.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per bottle": "26 g (label)",
      "Calories": "170",
      "Sugar": "5 g, 0 g added",
      "Best for": "No-mix evenings"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "owyn-pro-elite-protein-powder",
    "name": "OWYN Pro Elite Protein Powder",
    "brand": "OWYN",
    "badge": "Best Dairy-Free",
    "category": "Pea and pumpkin plant protein",
    "description": "There is no plant casein, but a large plant dose still works as a night snack. OWYN's chocolate panel at the time of writing lists 30 g of pea and pumpkin protein in 180 calories per two-scoop serving, with 0 g of sugar and monk fruit sweetening. The brand markets it as free of the major allergens, so it suits people who avoid dairy entirely and still want a substantial pre-sleep serving.",
    "pros": [
      "30 g of plant protein per serving, per the label",
      "0 g of sugar, monk fruit sweetened",
      "Free of dairy, soy and other major allergens, per the brand"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No pre-sleep research exists on pea-pumpkin blends specifically",
      "No NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status",
      "Two scoops per serving empties the tub faster"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for dairy-free readers who want a full 30 g dose at night.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "30 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 180",
      "Sugar": "0 g (label)",
      "Best for": "Dairy-free and vegan diets"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Protein Powder Before Bed",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-protein-powder-before-bed/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein powder to take before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, a micellar casein such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein. Casein digests slowly and is the protein almost all pre-sleep studies used. Ascent Micellar Casein is the certified option, and OWYN Pro Elite suits dairy-free diets. Whatever you choose, aim for 30 to 40 g of protein, not a single small scoop."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it good to drink a protein shake before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For people who train and want more protein, yes. Studies show casein taken before sleep is digested overnight and used for muscle repair, and adding a nightly shake improved gains in a 12-week trial. When total daily protein is matched, the timing advantage mostly disappears, so it is a convenient way to add protein rather than a requirement."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I take casein or whey before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Casein is the better default because it forms a gel in the stomach and releases amino acids slowly through the night. Whey digests quickly, but a full 30 to 40 g dose still provides protein for several hours. If you already own whey, a larger serving before bed is fine; if you are buying something new for evenings, choose casein."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein should I take before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The research points to 30 to 40 g. A 2017 study in older men found 40 g of casein raised overnight muscle protein synthesis while 20 g did not. Most casein scoops give 21 to 25 g, so plan on one and a half to two scoops. Smaller people can stay near 30 g; older adults should lean toward 40 g."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a protein shake before bed make me gain weight?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not by itself. Weight change depends on total calories over the day, and a casein shake in water adds roughly 120 to 240 calories. Small studies in active young people found no drop in next-morning fat burning. It only causes gain if the calories push you over your daily needs, so adjust elsewhere if you add one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does protein before bed help you sleep?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There is no good evidence that protein makes you fall asleep faster, despite claims about tryptophan in milk. The goal is a shake that does not disturb sleep: avoid caffeinated or coffee flavors, keep the volume moderate if you get reflux, and choose lactose-free or plant options if dairy upsets your stomach at night."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I take a plant protein before bed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. There is no plant casein and no published pre-sleep trial on pea or pumpkin protein, but a 30 g plant serving still adds meaningful protein to your day, which is the main way bedtime shakes help. OWYN Pro Elite gives 30 g in about 180 calories per its label. Anyone avoiding dairy should not feel they are missing out much."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Specialty Protein",
    tags: [
      "best protein powder before bed",
      "protein before bed",
      "casein before bed",
      "nighttime protein shake",
      "pre-sleep protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder Before Bed</Fragment>
      Casein, whey-casein blends and a dairy-free option compared on the numbers that matter at night: protein type, a 30-40 g dose, calories and nothing that keeps you awake.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#quick-comparison">Comparison Table</a>
      <a href="#how-we-picked">How We Picked</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#research">The Pre-Sleep Research</a>
      <a href="#casein-whey-blends">Casein vs Whey vs Blends</a>
      <a href="#dose">How Much Before Bed</a>
      <a href="#calories">Calories and Fat Loss</a>
      <a href="#sleep-friendly">Sleep-Friendly Shakes</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Can Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best protein powder before bed is a slow-digesting micellar casein taken at a 30 to 40 g dose, which usually means one and a half to two scoops rather than one. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein is the easiest way to do that, with 24 g of protein in about 120 calories per scoop on its label. Ascent Micellar Casein is the certified, stevia-sweetened alternative, Quest suits people who want one whey-casein tub for day and night, and OWYN Pro Elite covers dairy-free diets. The research behind a bedtime shake is real but narrower than marketing suggests: it works mostly because it adds protein to your day.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before-Bed Protein Powders Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            The answer first. These are the eight products we rank for a pre-sleep shake, with the label numbers that matter at night: protein per scoop, calories, sugar, a rough price tier and any third-party certification. Price tier reflects cost per 25 g of protein at typical retail prices when we checked: budget, mid or premium. Nutrition figures come from each brand's label or listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the panel on the tub you actually buy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Certification</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low, about 1-2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand banned-substance testing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ascent Micellar Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Sport (per brand)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Levels 100% Micellar Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Legion Casein+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Quest Protein Powder (Vanilla)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">BSN Syntha-6</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 190-200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fairlife Core Power 26 g (bottle)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g (0 g added)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">OWYN Pro Elite (2 scoops)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you only read one line, read this: a scoop and a half of micellar casein, about 36 g of protein, taken 30 to 60 minutes before bed is the setup the research most closely supports. Gold Standard Casein is the easiest way to do that. Ascent is the choice if you want certification and no artificial sweeteners, Quest suits people who want one tub for the whole day, and OWYN Pro Elite is the dairy-free option.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is about the bedtime use case specifically. If you want a deeper comparison of casein powders on their own merits, including micellar casein versus calcium caseinate and how each tub is made, our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide covers that ground in detail. Here we focus on what to drink before sleep, how much, and whether it is worth doing at all.
