        <div id="research" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the Pre-Sleep Protein Research Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            The modern case for a bedtime shake comes mostly from one group: Luc van Loon's lab at Maastricht University. In 2012, Res and colleagues gave 16 healthy young men either 40 g of casein or a placebo immediately before sleep, after an evening resistance-training session. The casein was digested and absorbed normally during the night, and whole-body protein balance overnight was better than with placebo. The key finding was simply that sleep does not stop you digesting protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Later work from the same group went further. Trommelen and colleagues used specially labeled casein to show that amino acids from a pre-sleep drink are actually built into muscle protein during overnight recovery, not just absorbed into the blood. In older men, Kouw and colleagues found in 2017 that 40 g of casein before sleep raised overnight muscle protein synthesis, while 20 g did not, which is one reason this guide treats 30 to 40 g as the useful range rather than a single scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The longer-term evidence is the 2015 training study by Snijders and colleagues. Young men did 12 weeks of resistance training and took either a drink with about 27.5 g of casein and some carbohydrate every night, or a non-caloric placebo. The protein group gained more strength and more muscle size. It is a well-run study and it is the one most often quoted by supplement marketers, but it comes with a significant caveat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The placebo group did not get the extra protein at any other time of day, so the casein group simply ate more protein in total. That makes it hard to say whether the timing mattered or the extra 27.5 g did. When researchers have matched total daily protein, the bedtime advantage has been much harder to find; a 2018 study by Joy and colleagues, for example, found that casein taken in the daytime or at night produced similar gains in muscle size and strength. Reviews of the field, including Trommelen and van Loon's own, frame pre-sleep protein as a practical way to add a protein meal rather than a magic window.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST SUMMARY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A bedtime shake works, mainly because it is extra protein</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Casein before sleep is digested overnight and feeds muscle repair, and studies that added a nightly shake saw better gains. Studies that matched total daily protein mostly did not. If you already hit your daily target, the bedtime timing is a small extra at best; if you fall short, it is one of the easiest places to add 30-40 g.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            So who benefits most? People who train in the evening, people with a long gap between dinner and breakfast, older adults whose muscles respond less to smaller protein doses, and anyone struggling to reach their daily total, which our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide helps you calculate. If your diet already supplies about 1.6 g per kg of body weight spread across several meals, a bedtime shake is optional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="casein-whey-blends" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Casein vs Whey vs Blends at Night
          </h2>

          <p>
            Casein is the classic bedtime protein because of how it behaves in the stomach. Micellar casein clots in stomach acid, forming a gel that empties slowly and releases amino acids over several hours. Whey stays liquid, empties fast and produces a sharp, short peak in blood amino acids. On paper, a slow trickle suits eight hours without food better than a quick spike, and almost all of the pre-sleep trials used casein for that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean whey before bed is useless. A large dose of whey still provides amino acids for several hours, and some studies have found that once total daily protein is matched, the type of protein matters far less than the amount. If you already own a tub of whey and do not want to buy anything else, a full 30 to 40 g serving of whey before bed is a perfectly reasonable choice. You simply lose some of the slow-release logic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blends are the middle ground. Products like Quest and Syntha-6 combine fast whey with slow casein, and milk itself is about 80% casein and 20% whey, which is why an ultra-filtered milk shake like Core Power behaves much like a casein product. Blends are the practical answer for people who want one tub for both post-workout and evening use. The trade-off is a little less casein per scoop than a pure casein powder.
          </p>

