        <div id="dose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Protein to Take Before Bed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The research points at 30 to 40 g. Res used 40 g, Kouw found 40 g worked in older men where 20 g did not, and the Snijders training study used about 27.5 g. Most single scoops of casein deliver 21 to 25 g, so the practical move is a scoop and a half or two scoops rather than one. The most common mistake is taking one small scoop and expecting the results of a 40 g study.
          </p>

          <p>
            Body size and age shift the target. A smaller woman who weighs 60 kg will get plenty from about 30 g; a 100 kg lifter may prefer the full 40 g. Older adults appear to need larger doses to get the same muscle response, so people over about 60 should lean toward the top of the range, which our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a> guide discusses further. Anyone with kidney disease should not add a large nightly protein dose without talking to their doctor first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Food works too. A 250 g tub of plain Greek yogurt or a cup and a half of cottage cheese delivers roughly 25 to 40 g of mostly casein protein, depending on the brand. If you would rather eat than drink before bed, that is a legitimate option with the same underlying logic. Powder simply wins on convenience, calories per gram of protein and the ability to make a thick pudding in a minute.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DOSE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">30-40 g of protein, 30-60 minutes before sleep</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That usually means 1.5 to 2 scoops of casein, not one. Older adults and larger lifters should aim for the top of the range; smaller people can stay near 30 g.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Calories, Weight and Fat Loss at Night
          </h2>

          <p>
            A common worry is that eating before bed makes you gain fat. Total calories across the day matter far more than when you eat them, and a 36 g casein shake mixed with water adds only about 180 calories. If it replaces late-night snacking, it can even help. If it is added on top of a full dinner and dessert, the extra calories count like any others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small studies from Florida State University looked at this directly. In active young people, a 30 to 40 g pre-sleep protein shake did not reduce next-morning fat burning, and some measurements of metabolism were slightly higher. Results in sedentary people with overweight were less clear-cut, with some showing higher morning insulin. None of these studies is large enough to settle the question, but together they suggest a moderate bedtime shake is not the fat-gain trap it is sometimes made out to be.
          </p>

          <p>
            The liquid you use changes the math quickly. Two scoops of casein in water is about 240 calories; the same two scoops in a cup of whole milk is closer to 390. If you are cutting, mix with water or unsweetened almond milk and make it thick with less liquid. If you are trying to gain weight, milk, a spoon of peanut butter or a blend like Syntha-6 adds useful calories at a time of day when most people are not otherwise eating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sleep-friendly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Keeping Your Bedtime Shake Sleep-Friendly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein itself is not a sleep aid, whatever some labels imply. Milk proteins contain tryptophan, the amino acid the body uses to make serotonin and melatonin, but there is no good evidence that a casein shake makes you fall asleep faster. The realistic goal is a shake that does not disrupt sleep, and that comes down to a handful of practical choices.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, avoid caffeine. Coffee and mocha flavors, "energy" or "thermogenic" protein products and some pre-workout-style blends contain added caffeine, sometimes 100 mg or more per scoop. Chocolate flavors contain a small amount of naturally occurring caffeine from cocoa, usually far less than a cup of tea, which most people tolerate fine. If you are sensitive, a vanilla or unflavored casein removes the question entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, watch the volume and the timing if you have reflux. A large shake drunk right before lying down can trigger heartburn in people prone to it. A thick casein pudding made with less liquid, eaten 45 to 60 minutes before bed, is easier on the stomach than 500 ml of liquid gulped at the last minute. If a full serving gives you reflux, split it between after dinner and later in the evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, mind your digestion. If lactose bothers you, dairy shakes taken late can mean gas and bloating at exactly the wrong time. Casein powders contain some lactose, so lactose-free milk products like Core Power, or a plant protein like OWYN, are better choices. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">best protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide covers other triggers, including sugar alcohols and gums, that are worth avoiding at night.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Can Skip a Bedtime Protein Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plenty of people do not need one. If you eat three or four protein-rich meals a day and comfortably reach your target, a pre-sleep shake is unlikely to change your results noticeably. The research that matched total protein suggests timing is a minor factor compared with the daily total and consistent training.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people should check with a professional first. Anyone with kidney disease, anyone on a protein-restricted diet, people with diabetes whose medication is timed around meals, and anyone pregnant or managing a medical condition should talk to their doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a regular nightly shake. This guide is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the decision is practical. If a nightly casein pudding helps you hit your protein target, curbs late-night snacking or fits naturally after evening training, it is a sensible habit with modest but real support. If it is a chore you keep forgetting, put the same protein into breakfast instead and you will lose very little. Our guide to <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> walks through the other timing options.
          </p>
        </div>

